{"ts":"2026-11-12T10:23:22.154Z","actor":"claude","stage":"factcheck","action":"factcheck.review","details":{"verdict":"pass","notes":"Checked the geometry independently: opposition instant 19 Feb 2027 16:44 CET, Mars opposite the Sun, up all night, magnitude -1.2. At 00:00 on 18/19 Feb Mars is 55 deg up in the south (visible-mars fact), so 'high in the south around midnight' is right, and the planet's non-twinkling appearance is standard. Earth-Mars distance at opposition recomputed as 101.4 million km; the 2026-2029 scan confirms the minimum is 101.4 million on 20 Feb 2027, so 'as close and as bright as it gets' is true for this cycle. NASA sourced range 55.8 (2003 closest) to 401 million km (farthest) is quoted exactly. The script says opposition is 'the moment we are on the same side of the Sun', which is the correct definition, and the description flags the 3D orbit view as not to scale. No unsupported brightness ranking: Mars at -1.2 is fainter than Sirius, so I deliberately avoided 'brightest object' claims."},"gitSha":"395ad8b"}
